# Line endings are normalised on the way into the repository.
#
# Every text file is stored with LF regardless of the platform it was
# written on, and checked out with LF. Without this, a tool that rewrites a
# whole file on Windows can commit it as CRLF, and git records that as every
# line being deleted and re-added — a change of one line reads as a change of
# eight hundred, and it conflicts with every other branch touching the file.
#
# **The formatter does not cover this.** `format:check` runs Prettier, which
# refuses files it has no parser for — `prisma/schema.prisma` among them,
# where it exits with "No parser could be inferred" and the file is skipped
# entirely. So the one class of file most likely to be rewritten wholesale by
# a migration tool is exactly the class the formatter never inspects. This
# rule is enforced by git itself, at the point the blob is written, and so it
# does not depend on any check remembering to look.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, byte-for-byte. Listing these is what stops `text=auto` from
# guessing on a file whose contents happen to look textual.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
